rom did the bottle leak no the reason  there's little droplets of water is  because that water bottle that soda  bottle is really cold you just took it  out of the refrigerator and so the  moisture that's in the air came into  contact moose Chur in the air came into  contact with that cold bottle and it  condensed forming little liquid droplets  well if we relate this to the water  cycle  by relating this to the water cycle that  is how we see clouds form clouds are the  other results of condensation water  vapor will condense and form clouds and  when the liquid water of clouds gets  heavy enough of course it will rain and  so when we look at the next stage of the  water cycle we have precipitation and in  the animation we have water h2o falling  out of the cloud well when we look at  precipitation there's different types of  precipitation most obvious being this  picture here of rain here's another  picture of precipitation snow and here's  a form of precipitation called sleet and  if you're not familiar with sleet it's  kind of like a frozen rain sleet is  actually very slippery and hard to drive  on because it's also you might hear the  phrase black ice because when water  freezes on a on a road you can't even  really see it and so the road might look  clear but it's really a real thin layer  of ice on the road very slippery hard to  drive on be careful and then a fourth  kind of hit of precipitation called hail  little small balls of ice that fall from  clouds and here we see a person holding  some hail the in their hand now the ones  in this picture here are are not too  common the two the usual size of hail is  smaller than peas so the hail balls are  usually not as large as the picture here  but when they are they can cause some  damage you look at this picture right  here  you can see the back window of this  automobile broken because of hail  damaged  so now that waters on the ground so it's  been raining or sleet or some kind of  precipitation now that water's on the  ground what happens next well water is  going to do one of two things  notice in the animation water is simply  running downhill and that's simply  called runoff so when water hits the  ground it's going to roll downhill into  a river into a lake into an ocean water  is going to roll downhill into its  lowest point which eventually is  probably going to be one of the oceans  well as water is running downhill it  also might soak into the ground and  that's what the word infiltration means  when water hits the ground it can soak  into the soil and this will create  what's called groundwater so in our  animation we have some h2o s  accumulating under the ground and this  is where a lot of the of the world gets  their drinking water from is from  groundwater let's look at that really  quickly in a little more detail  when we look at this picture here we can  see kind of a cutaway of the surface of  the earth and then underneath the  surface we see the flow of groundwater  well what we often do is we'll drill  wells down into the groundwater supply  and that will supply drinking water and  cleaning water and bathing water  well you might wonder well if we drill  down in mik wells won't we run out of  groundwater well the reason why we don't  run out of groundwater is because of  infiltration when water soaks into the  ground infiltration will replenish or  recharge the groundwater supply so let's  come back to our animation here let's  just kind of show all the steps at once  so here we have evaporation water  evaporating from this pond and now I've  added transpiration water evaporating  from the leaves of the tree and so as  the water goes up eventually it's going  to form clouds and clouds are the  results of condensation when the gas of  when the water gas turns back into a  liquid gas I said that wrong when water  gas turns back into a liquid that's  called condensation and eventually that  liquid gets heavy enough where it falls  as precipitation whether it's snow rain  sleet or hail and once water is on the  ground let's say it's rain once it rains  water is going to do one of two things  once it's on the ground it's either  going to run off downhill or it's going  to soak into the soil but if you look in  the animation here we have a cycle a  continuous around the clock cycle here  known as the hydrologic cycle or the  water cycle so I'm going to just let  this play for a few moments before we  conclude with a little practice quiz  you  okay so here we have a practice quiz go  ahead and pause the video and if you're  in my class try to answer these  questions on a separate sheet of paper  and bring them to me I'd love to check  them for accuracy so go ahead and pause  the video and try to answer these good  luck
 

